  "strategy": "Physical Analogy and Approach Rationale:\n\nWe model the function h(x) as a probability distribution on the interval [0,2] with total area 1. The overlap integral C5 measures the maximum probability of finding h(x) and h(x+k) both executing their functions simultaneously. The minimal C5 corresponds to a distribution where shifts of h(x) do not significantly overlap.\n\nThe central idea is to find a periodic pattern at multiple resolutions and then refine it via gradient-based optimization. We reduce dimensionality by enforcing harmony conditions (phase consistency) and rhythmic repetition (fixed periods), then minimize C5 under stability constraints.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nVariables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2]\nConstraints: \u222bh(x)dx = 1, h(2) = h(0) (periodic boundary)\nObjective: mine_{h with harmony} max_{k\u2208Z} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nWe discretize with n=320 grid points. The representation is the grid values h[i]: [0,1]^320. We enforce constraints through equality constraints in optimization.\n\nWe introduce periodicity conditions and discrete harmony operators. Candidates satisfy a consistency relation.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. Mothership Construction:\n   - Start with n=320 grid cycles\n   - Initialize candidate solution via zero-padded Wherry harmonics\n   - Repair to integral=1 using contraction/expansion factors\n\n2. Multi-resonance Refinement:\n   - Downscale candidate to half-grid (n//2) preserving approximate area\n   - Apply symmetry constraint to enforce phase relations\n   - Reparameterize to positive/negative frequency components\n\n3. Solver-driven evolution:\n   - Use exponential smoothing on gradient descent steps\n   - Employ Lagrangian multipliers for constraints\n   - Include regularization term proportional to grid frequency\n   - Track best candidate separately\n\n4. Continuity maintenance:\n   - At coarser grids, use convex interpolation\n   - Preserve maxima at grid transitions\n\n5. Phase-tying operation:\n   - Decompose candidate via wavelet packet analysis\n   - Regenerate harmonically consistent subcomponents\n\nSearch Choices:\n\n- Computational budget: 1100s wall time with 1 CPU\n- Primary algorithm: Projected Gradient with Line Search\n- Parametric sweep: Period length P \u2208 {14,21,28,35}\n- Secondary updates: Local search with momentum, variable transformation frequency\n\nBudget Estimation:\n- Initial construction: O(n log n) time\n- DCGM solver: O(n^2) operations per evaluation\n- Total evaluations \u2264 30 \u2192 15000 n^2 ~ 7.68e9 operations \u2192 7680s estimated, well within budget\n\nTermination Condition:\n- C5 reduction below 0.3800 or 100 improvement steps\n- If stalled, restart with neighboring state\n\nRepair and Validation:\n- Project gradients to tangent space of constraint manifold\n- Integral normalization via weighted averaging\n- Ensure h(x) in [0,1] through clipping\n- Exact score via np.correlate()\n\nEvaluators: \n- _eval(h): max over lags of (correlation dot product) * (2/n)\n- repair(h): clip+normalize (n0/2 = sum(h))\n\nContinuation:\n- Successful dimensions: 320, default P=35\n- \u03c6 = golden angle 360/\u03c6^2 \u2248 218.01402",
